The native key behind each key event (Task 14 of docs/plans/20261014-gpui-frontend.md): GPUI's `Keystroke` keeps
neither a key's position nor its layout's unshifted character and drops Shift from symbols, which a terminal needs for
physical-key bindings and the Kitty keyboard protocol. The Wayland and X11 clients install a `NativeInput` for exactly
the dispatch of one key event (an RAII scope): a press, each of its autorepeats (Wayland's timer with the press's own
record; on X11 a press of the key still held) and its release carry `NativeInput::Key` (XKB keycode, modifiers with
Shift, consumed modifiers, Caps Lock and Num Lock, the layout's level-0 character and whether that layout types ASCII,
the text before Ctrl transformations, composition and repeat flags, the keystroke GPUI built); text-input-v3's one-character commit, dispatched as a synthetic key, carries
`NativeInput::Text`. `current_native_input()` reads it during the dispatch. And a `text/html` clipboard offer:
`with_clipboard_html(ClipboardHtml { html, with_text }, write)` makes the clipboard writes inside `write` offer the
HTML as `text/html` beside the item's text, or alone (Ghostty's `copy_to_clipboard:mixed` and `:html`), from the
Wayland data source and the X11 selection owner. Linux only and additive: no GPUI type changes, so gpui-kit,
gpui-component and the other platforms build unchanged. scripts/vendor-gpui.sh applies this to the verified
crates.io archive.

